  "summary": "Imperial court robe featuring dragon motifs worn by Manchu emperors and high-ranking officials",
  "description": "Characterized by its bright colors, symbolic dragon motifs, and structured cut with side slits. Created to display imperial power and cosmic harmony, incorporating Manchu riding elements with Han Chinese symbolic imagery.",
  "culturalContext": "The Qing dragon robe (jifu) — emperor and imperial family's ceremonial dress — encoded cosmic order in silk: nine five-clawed dragons (\"nine-five\" = supreme sovereignty), twelve symbols of sovereignty, lishui striped hem waves, and cloud-collar bands, all regulated by the imperial wardrobe (Shangsi yuan) inventories. Forbidden City collections preserve the canon; film and drama (The Last Emperor) fixed its image as \"Chinese emperor\" worldwide, and court-textile studies (Valmaret Gary Dickinson) document its grammar.",
